Behaviors matched exactly:[0m[0m
- **Call/output order**: [38;5;10mgood()[0m → [38;5;10mgoodG2B[0m (hard-coded [38;5;10mdata = 7[0m, no stdin) then [38;5;10mgoodB2G[0m, then [38;5;10mbad()[0m. So only two [38;5;10mfgets[0m reads happen: the first input line goes to [38;5;10mgoodB2G[0m, the second to [38;5;10mbad()[0m.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mfgets(buf, 14, stdin)[0m**: at most 13 bytes, stops after a retained newline, leftovers stay in the stream for the next call (verified with a 17-char first line splitting across the two reads). NULL only on EOF-with-nothing-read → [38;5;10m"fgets() failed."[0m and [38;5;10mdata[0m stays −1.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10matoi[0m**: leading whitespace, optional sign, digit run; modeled as glibc's [38;5;10m(int)strtol(...)[0m, so it saturates at the [38;5;10mlong[0m bound then truncates to [38;5;10mi32[0m.[0m[0m
- **Check order and messages**: [38;5;10mbad()[0m checks only [38;5;10mdata >= 0[0m ([38;5;10m"ERROR: Array index is negative."[0m); [38;5;10mgoodB2G[0m checks [38;5;10m0 <= data < 10[0m ([38;5;10m"ERROR: Array index is out-of-bounds"[0m, no period, as in the C).[0m[0m
