[0m[0m
The defect is preserved: [38;5;10mbad()[0m has no upper-bound check. For every index I could compare (0–1000), the C program writes past [38;5;10mbuffer[0m into unrelated stack storage and still prints ten zeros with exit 0; the Rust port reproduces that byte-for-byte by discarding the out-of-range write. Beyond roughly index 1000–5000 the C write leaves the mapped stack and the process dies with SIGSEGV, losing its unflushed stdout buffer entirely. That threshold is undefined behavior and shifts with stack layout, ASLR, and environment size, so the Rust version does not crash there — it prints the ten zeros and exits 0. This is the only known output divergence, and it's noted in a comment at the top of [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m.[0m[0m
